  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "The lines $y=7x-2$ and $kx-14y=7$ are perpendicular. What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\n- The first line is in slope-intercept form, and the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So the slope of the line $y=7x-2$ is $7.$\n\n- To find the slope of $kx-14y=7,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kx-14y & =7 \\\\ 14y & =kx-7 \\\\ y & =\\frac{k}{14}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line $kx-14y=7$ is $\\frac{k}{14}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n7\\cdot (\\frac{k}{14})=-1\\,\u27f9\\,k=-2.\n$$",
